-- ============================================================================
-- BMS — Tanzania address reference data for a newly provisioned tenant database
-- ============================================================================
-- Generated 2026-09-08. Applied by core/tenant_provisioner.php immediately
-- after schema/tenant_seed_defaults.sql, as its own separate step/file.
--
-- WHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM tenant_seed_defaults.sql: that file is small and
-- hand-curated — every row earns its place there, and several categories are
-- deliberately EXCLUDED for privacy (see its own header). This file is the
-- opposite: large (~33,700 rows), never hand-edited, and contains nothing but
-- public Tanzania administrative geography — the same data is correct for
-- every tenant, unconditionally, with zero privacy concerns. Keeping the two
-- apart means a future review of the curated accounts/permissions seed never
-- has to scroll past tens of thousands of village names to get there.
--
-- WHAT IS IN HERE, and why every row is safe to seed identically everywhere:
--   countries   reference list (10 rows) — Tanzania and its neighbours
--   regions     Tanzania's regions (31 rows)
--   districts   Tanzania's districts (178 rows)
--   wards       Tanzania's wards (3,997 rows)
--   villages    Tanzania's villages (29,472 rows)
-- None of these carry a tenant's own data — they are the address dropdowns
-- (country -> region -> district -> ward -> village) used across Customer,
-- Supplier, and Sub-Contractor forms. Without this file, a newly provisioned
-- tenant has the correct TABLES (schema/tenant_schema_template.sql already
-- creates them) but zero rows in them, so those dropdowns are empty — reported
-- live 2026-09-08.
--
-- FORMAT: extended (multi-row) INSERT statements, chunked to roughly 256KB
-- per statement (not one row per statement, and not all 29,472 villages in a
-- single statement either) — this file is applied as one blocking step during
-- every new company's signup (core/tenant_provisioner.php's apply_geography
-- step), so statement size matters for both provisioning speed and staying
-- safely under a MySQL server's max_allowed_packet regardless of how a given
-- deployment has it configured. Explicit column names on every statement
-- (mysqldump --complete-insert), not positional VALUES, so this file survives
-- unaffected if tenant_schema_template.sql's column order ever drifts from
-- the source database's.
--
-- REGENERATING (e.g. after Tanzania's own administrative boundaries change):
--   "/path/to/mysqldump" -uUSER -pPASS -hHOST --no-create-info --skip-triggers \
--     --complete-insert --extended-insert --net_buffer_length=262144 \
--     --skip-comments --compact SOURCE_DB countries regions districts wards villages \
--     > schema/tenant_geography_seed.sql
--   Then re-add this header (mysqldump --compact strips it) and re-run
--   tests/test_tenant_geography_seed_cli.php to confirm row counts still match.
-- ============================================================================

SET @OLD_FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = @@FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS;
SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = 0;
SET NAMES utf8mb4;

